---
import BlogArticleShell from '../../../components/BlogArticleShell.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const canonicalPath = '/blog/eu-product-identifiers-pid-spreadsheet-buyers-2026';
const publishedISO = '2026-07-04';
---

<BlogArticleShell
  title="EU product identifiers (PID): spreadsheet buyer guide for 2026 | kakobuy-cn.com"
  description="From 1 November 2026, EU e-commerce imports require product identifiers. Learn what M-PID, NS-PID, and S-PID mean for Kakobuy Spreadsheet buyers and parcel traceability."
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  headline="EU product identifiers (PID): why spreadsheet row hygiene matters before November 2026"
  publishedISO={publishedISO}
  displayDate="4 July 2026"
  structuredKeywords={[
    'EU product identifiers PID 2026',
    'M-PID Kakobuy Spreadsheet',
    'merchant product identifier customs',
    'EU e-commerce import traceability',
    'spreadsheet SKU hygiene 2026',
  ]}
>
  <p class="blog-lede" slot="lede">
    Product identifiers are becoming part of the EU customs story, and that matters for buyers who discover items through the <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet</a></strong>.
    EU guidance says Product Identifiers can be declared voluntarily from <strong>1 July 2026</strong> and become mandatory for distance-sale imports from <strong>1 November 2026</strong>.
    For shoppers, the practical lesson is not to memorise TARIC codes; it is to keep cleaner product records.
  </p>

  <h2>What is a PID?</h2>
  <p>
    EU guidance around the 2026 low-value import reform describes Product Identifiers as traceability fields for e-commerce goods.
    The most important one for many online purchases is the <strong>Merchant Product Identifier</strong>, or M-PID, which is assigned by the online seller, marketplace, or platform.
    Related fields include manufacturer product identifiers when available.
    Source:
    <a href="https://taxation-customs.ec.europa.eu/news/guidance-and-legal-text-temporary-flat-fee-low-value-imports-which-will-apply-until-1-july-2028-2026-06-08_en" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU guidance on the temporary flat fee and PIDs</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why buyers should care even if carriers file the data</h2>
  <p>
    Kakobuy users are not usually filing customs declarations themselves.
    Still, the quality of the upstream listing data affects how easy it is for a carrier, marketplace, or customs system to understand the goods.
    If a row disappears, a seller changes the listing, or a product has several near-identical variants, your screenshots and order notes become part of the evidence trail.
  </p>

  <div class="blog-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Identifier idea</th><th scope="col">Buyer-friendly translation</th><th scope="col">What to save</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>M-PID</td><td>Seller/platform product code or SKU</td><td>Listing URL, item code, seller option</td></tr>
        <tr><td>NS-PID</td><td>Manufacturer/supplier internal code</td><td>Model number if shown</td></tr>
        <tr><td>S-PID</td><td>Standard barcode-style identifier</td><td>Barcode/EAN/GTIN if present</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No clear identifier</td><td>Higher ambiguity</td><td>More screenshots and careful remarks</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Spreadsheet row hygiene checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Save the original product URL before purchase.</li>
    <li>Screenshot the selected size, colour, batch, or seller option.</li>
    <li>Record the row category: <a href="/category/shoes/">Shoes</a>, <a href="/category/bags/">Bags</a>, <a href="/category/electronics/">Electronics</a>, or another lane.</li>
    <li>Keep the Kakobuy order number and seller tracking status together.</li>
    <li>If the product has a visible model number or SKU, copy it into your personal haul note.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why variants are the hidden risk</h2>
  <p>
    Product identifiers are meant to distinguish goods, but rep and agent-shopping listings often contain many variants under one seller page.
    A single page might include budget and premium batches, multiple colours, and several size systems.
    That is why the <a href="/blog/spreadsheet-wrong-variant-recovery-2026/">wrong variant recovery guide</a> remains relevant.
    Cleaner records make it easier to explain what you intended to buy if warehouse QC does not match the order.
  </p>

  <h2>How this connects to EU parcel duty</h2>
  <p>
    The PID requirement sits beside the <a href="/blog/eu-low-value-parcel-duty-kakobuy-spreadsheet-2026/">€3 low-value parcel duty</a> and <a href="/blog/eu-ics2-pre-arrival-data-kakobuy-parcels-2026/">ICS2 pre-arrival data</a>.
    Together, they show that EU customs is moving from loose parcel descriptions toward item-level traceability.
    A mixed Kakobuy haul should therefore have item-level notes before it reaches the international shipping screen.
  </p>

  <h2>What not to overdo</h2>
  <p>
    You do not need a corporate compliance database for a personal haul.
    Do not invent codes, do not paste unrelated barcodes, and do not claim manufacturer identifiers you cannot verify.
    The buyer-safe approach is simpler: save the seller URL, selected variant, Kakobuy order number, and QC evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    PIDs are a customs-system topic, but the buyer habit is easy to understand.
    Treat every spreadsheet row like a record you may need to explain later.
    The more precise your row notes are before shipping, the less fragile the parcel becomes when EU data requirements tighten.
  </p>
</BlogArticleShell>
